--- body ---
## Context

The journal article (`publications/journal-article/paper_benchmark_merged.md`)
asks "can AI produce economic statistics?" and benchmarks it on thermal
plants in Vietnam. Section 2 (Related Work) is presently skeletal: three
subsection stubs with HTML-comment content plans, no prose, few citations
woven in. This ticket fills that section.

The operator's convention: **no heading above a one-paragraph subsection.**
So Section 2 becomes four flat paragraphs (not §2.1/§2.2/§2.3/§2.4
headings), covering — in order — LLM evaluation for structured
generation, RAG for knowledge-intensive tasks, AI for economic/energy
data, and a closing paragraph that states the gap this benchmark fills.
The gap paragraph must not over-sell: we do not claim exhaustive
knowledge of prior art — we state what we could not find and what
this paper adds.

But the paragraphs alone are not enough for author due diligence. Each
cited work must be *known*, not merely *listed*. For each of the four
paragraphs, the operator needs a deeper **due-diligence note** that:
- explains each cited work with enough detail to confirm intellectual
  relevance (what the paper actually did, why we cite it, what claim it
  supports);
- explains related-but-not-cited works with less detail and justifies
  their non-selection (superseded, off-topic, redundant, weaker
  evidence);
- gives general history-of-science context in that sub-domain so the
  operator is not blind to lineage;
- is **deep-researched** — fresh 2024–2026 results incorporated, not
  just older anchors;
- carries a **methods** section describing how it was written (searches
  run, databases, cutoff date, LLM-assist acknowledged);
- carries a **relevance** section justifying why this note exists for
  this paragraph of this paper;
- has a formal header (title, author = "Claude prompted by
  Ha-Duong Minh", date) and a formal bibliography with DOIs and URLs.

The note-production workflow is owned by **ticket 0098** (a reusable
skill `.claude/skills/related-work-note/SKILL.md`). This ticket
*consumes* that skill; it does not build it.

This ticket's deliverables:
1. Four notes produced by the 0098 skill, one per paragraph (three
   topical + one gap).
2. Four rewritten paragraphs in `paper_benchmark_merged.md` §2.
3. Any missing BibTeX entries added to `report/refs.bib`.

This ticket is blocked by 0098 until the skill is committed and
approved.

## Scope (what is in / what is out)

**In scope.**
- Paragraph 1: LLM evaluation for structured data generation.
  Anchors: standard QA/reasoning benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag,
  BIG-bench), information-extraction benchmarks (DocRED, SciREX,
  REBEL), table-understanding (TabFact, WikiTableQuestions,
  TableBench). Claim: established benchmarks test QA, reasoning,
  or factual recall, not the production of a *structured
  statistical table* against a gold reference.
- Paragraph 2: RAG for knowledge-intensive tasks.
  Anchors: Lewis et al. 2020 (ULQGRISS); a RAG survey (Gao et
  al. or similar); self-RAG, corrective RAG; agentic/deep-research
  systems (2024–2026 wave). Claim: RAG is evaluated for QA and
  factual grounding, not exhaustive dataset construction.
- Paragraph 3: AI for economic and energy data.
  Anchors: Gotzens et al. 2019 (LYGDCQCL); powerplantmatching;
  Global Power Plant Database (WRI); Global Energy Monitor
  trackers (LCVI7NAT); PyPSA-Earth; PyPSA-VN (VGASHFPT). These
  are **closely-related projects and must be cited.** Claim:
  existing energy databases are hand-curated; we have not found
  a prior benchmark that evaluates AI-assisted production of
  such tables.
- Paragraph 4: the gap, stated without over-selling.
  What we did not find in the literature, what our benchmark
  adds, and a forward pointer to §3 (benchmark design) and §7
  (discussion). No exhaustive-absence claim; the language is
  "to our knowledge" or "we did not find", not "nobody has done".

**Citation budget: strict 15 across all four paragraphs.** The
reader prefers a *focused* selection. Each paragraph mixes
**seminal** (field-defining), **review/survey** (for the reader
to drill down), and **recent** (2024–2026 frontier) works. The
skill's note for each paragraph proposes more; the ticket's job
is to cut to 15 with reasons. Roughly 4–5 refs per topical
paragraph, 0–1 in the gap paragraph (gap paragraph mainly
forward-references this paper's later sections).

**No self-citation of in-repo lit reviews.** Do not cite
`docs/quality-grounding.md` in §2. The operator's convention:
quality-framework material is handled *in-paper* by forward
references — "see §3.3" or "as discussed in §7" — not by
pointing readers to a working document.

**Out of scope.**
- A standalone `docs/` lit-review document. Dropped in the
  2026-04-16 rescope; superseded by this plan.
- The quality-frameworks / philosophy-of-science strand.
  Material exists in `docs/quality-grounding.md` but is **not**
  cited from §2. If it surfaces in the paper at all, it is via
  §3.3 (Metrics) or §7 (Discussion), developed inline.
- Prompt-optimization frameworks (DSPy, TextGrad, OPRO). Owned
  by ticket 0075.
- Agentic-pipeline frameworks (LangGraph, Deep Agents). Owned by
  ticket 0076. Paragraph 2 may mention "agentic RAG / deep
  research" as an evaluation-literature trend but does not
  endorse or adopt any framework.
- The PyPSA-ASEAN 60-cell scaling argument. Lives in §1 or §7,
  not §2. §2 serves only the Vietnam-thermal benchmark framing.

## Actions

1. **Verify ticket 0098 is closed** (skill exists and is approved).
2. **Invoke the 0098 skill four times** (freshly each time — no
   cached reuse; freshness cutoff must reflect the invocation date),
   once per paragraph. Output lands in
   `publications/journal-article/notes/02_related_work/`:
   - `01_llm_benchmarks_structured_extraction.md`
   - `02_rag_knowledge_intensive.md`
   - `03_ai_economic_energy_data.md`
   - `04_gap_and_positioning.md`
   Each note is self-contained and stands on its own for an author
   who wants to verify a citation before defending it. Each note
   must pass the structural checklist defined in 0098.
3. **Cut to strict 15 citations across all four paragraphs.** The
   skill's notes will propose more. Reduce. Record which candidates
   were dropped and why in each note's "Related but not cited" list
   (moves up from the skill's proposal to the author's deliberate
   choice). Mix per topical paragraph: one seminal + one review/
   survey + two or three recent. Paragraph 4 (gap) carries 0–1
   citations; the rest is forward references ("see §3", "see §7").
4. **Write the four paragraphs** into `paper_benchmark_merged.md`
   §2. Replace the current HTML-comment stubs. **No subsection
   headings** (operator convention: no heading above a one-paragraph
   subsection). 200–300 words each. The gap paragraph states what
   we did not find and what this paper adds — no exhaustive-absence
   claim.
5. **Update `report/refs.bib`** with every cited work that is not
   already present. Prefer existing Zotero-style keys (see
   `memory/reference_zotero.md`); mint new keys otherwise. Every
   added entry must have DOI or stable URL.
6. **Operator review** of all four notes and the four paragraphs.
   Operator approves before merge.

## Test (first thing to do once 0098 is merged)

Run the skill on paragraph 1 (LLM benchmarks + structured
extraction). The note must pass the structural checklist owned by
0098, AND — once paragraph 1 is drafted — these deterministic
checks hold:

- [ ] The set of BibTeX keys cited in paragraph 1 equals the set
      of keys in the paragraph-1 note's "Cited works" bibliography
      (exact set equality, not subset).
- [ ] Every cited key resolves to an entry in `report/refs.bib`
      that has a DOI or stable URL.
- [ ] Paragraph 1 uses at most 5 citations.
- [ ] Paragraph 1's citation mix includes ≥1 seminal, ≥1 review/
      survey, and ≥1 recent (2024+) — author judgment call;
      recorded explicitly in the note.
- [ ] No citation points to `docs/quality-grounding.md` or any
      in-repo working document.

If paragraph 1 clears these, paragraphs 2, 3, 4 follow.

## Exit criteria

- Four notes under
  `publications/journal-article/notes/02_related_work/` committed,
  each passing 0098's structural checklist plus the paragraph-fit
  checks above.
- Four paragraphs in `paper_benchmark_merged.md` §2 (flat, no
  subsection headings), 200–300 words each, citing only works
  that appear in the corresponding note's "Cited works" list.
- **Exactly 15** unique citation keys across §2.
- Gap paragraph uses "we did not find / to our knowledge", not
  "nobody has done".
- Forward references ("see §3", "see §7") present where in-paper
  development replaces external citation.
- Closely-related projects (PyPSA-VN, PyPSA-Earth, powerplantmatching,
  GEM, GPPDB) are cited in paragraph 3.
- `report/refs.bib` updated; every new entry has DOI or stable
  URL; no duplicate keys across the four notes (if two paragraphs
  need Gotzens, they point to the same key).
- Operator has reviewed and approved the four notes and the
  paragraphs.

## Relationship to other tickets

- **0075** — prompt-optimization survey. Also produced
  `docs/quality-grounding.md`, which §2 does **not** cite
  (operator rule: no in-repo working documents in the paper's
  related work). The ideas in that doc may surface in §3.3 or §7
  via inline development. Non-overlapping scope.
- **0076** — LangChain Deep Agents. Non-overlapping; any agentic-RAG
  references in paragraph 2 are evaluation context, not framework
  adoption.
- **0054 / 0059** — multi-agent verification. Closed as negative
  result; not cited in §2 because the benchmark section doesn't
  discuss verification architectures (that's §6 Error Analysis /
  §7 Discussion territory).
